  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1237 PM MDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nWe continue to watch the potential for showers and isolated\nthunderstorms across the central mountains through Sunday\nmorning. There will be a potential for some locally heavier\nrainfall, especially tonight. That said, a more prolonged and\nwidespread issue with heavy rain is not expected. We will let\nthe FLASH FLOOD WATCH for the Wapiti burn scar expire this\nevening. Across the rest of eastern Idaho, the dry slot remains\nin place along with gusty winds at times during the same period.\nGusts up to 30-35 mph are possible, especially at night where\ndownslope occurs or where areas remain above any weak inversion,\nand during the afternoon with full mixing. Temperatures will\nwarm up a bit, with some low to mid 80s popping up across lowest\nelevations. \n\nThe focus for the next chance of showers and storms\nwill occur later Sunday through Labor Day. The low that has \nbeen sitting along the coast will swing across the state on \nMonday. Ahead of that, we will pull in some monsoon moisture to \nhelp fuel this round of showers and storms. It looks like at the\nmoment, any moisture from Marie will get pushed to our \nsoutheast. We will likely get a small boost, especially closer \nto the Wyoming border. Otherwise, rainfall will come from \nwhatever moisture is wrapping around the main low \nitself...separate from the monsoon push. The best chance of rain\nwill be Sunday night and Labor Day, with a very strong dry slot\nwrapping back into eastern Idaho later in the day. Rainfall \namounts across the Snake Plain, Magic Valley, and South \nHills/Albion Mountains will range from a few hundredths to close\nto 0.25\" north of Idaho Falls. Rainfall across the central \nmountains should range from 0.10-0.30\", EXCEPT across the higher\nranges (Sawtooths, Lost River, and Lemhi) where up 0.60\" is \npossible. Temperatures may be cold enough Monday, for a shorter \nperiod of rain and snow across the higher peaks like Borah, etc.\nFor the eastern and southeast highlands, amounts currently fall\nin the 0.20-0.50\"...locally higher around Island Park where up \nto 0.70\" MIGHT fall. If we see heavier storms or multiple storms\nover the same area, we could see some issue with localized \nflooding in steeper slope areas and burn scars. It is possible \nwe may need to issue a FLASH FLOOD WATCH to later on to cover \nthe impacts. Winds will pick up on the back side of the low, \nespecially when the dry slot moves in. The strongest winds will \nbe across the Snake Plain north across the border into southwest\nMontana. It is possible a WIND ADVISORY is needed for those \nareas. We should fall short of any thresholds for a LAKE WIND \nADVISORY for American Falls Reservoir. Temperatures Sunday will \nbe similar to tomorrow, but much cooler Monday with lower \nelevations highs MAYBE reaching 70. Mountain temperatures will \nbe in the 50s and low 60s, with highest elevations not getting \nout of the 40s. \n\nWe get a brief break midweek, but southwest flow returns and we\nsee some monsoon moisture moving back into state after that.\nExpect highs to return to the 70s and 80s and at least a low end\npotential (for now) of showers and thunderstorms.",
